Q: No matching function call to &#39;stoi&#39; in Qt A: <p>The signature of <code>stoi</code> is <code>int stoi(const std::string&amp; str, std::size_t* pos = nullptr, int base = 10)</code>.</p>
<p>The second parameter is an output pointer (<code>std::size_t*</code>) where <code>stoi</code> writes how many characters it consumed (see <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/string/basic_string/stol" rel="noreferrer">std::stoi</a>). When you pass <code>1</code>, the compiler tries to convert the <code>int 1</code> into a <code>std::size_t*</code>.</p>
<p>You can do the following instead. Note, the second argument is a <code>nullptr</code>, since you don't care about the output:</p>
<pre><code>int R = std::stoi(colorCString.substr(1, 2), nullptr, 16);
int G = std::stoi(colorCString.substr(3, 2), nullptr, 16);
int B = std::stoi(colorCString.substr(5, 2), nullptr, 16);
</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, to parse hex strings in Qt, you can use <a href="https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qcolor.html" rel="noreferrer"><code>QColor</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>QColor color(colorString);
if (color.isValid()) {
    int R = color.red();
    int G = color.green();
    int B = color.blue();
}
</code></pre>
